== Output files
<sdocs-output-files>
This behaviour is handled by the `filename`/`savepath` methods of the
`OutputFiles` enum of the `utils_enum.py` module,
#link(<sdocs-src.seiche.utils_enum.OutputFiles>)[see here]. The following
nomenclature is very far from perfect, and could benefit from some
renaming.

#figure(
  align(center)[#table(
    columns: 2,
    align: (right,auto,),
    table.header([file name], [description],),
    table.hline(),
    [dem\_\*.tif], [The Digital Elevation Model (DEM) used for the
    study.],
    [max\_speed\_\*.tif], [Single band GeoTiff raster holding the
    interpolated maximum water speed for the event (only relevant for
    .slf files). These files\' information are copied in there
    respective band of the hzd\_\*.tif files.],
    [duration\_\*.tif], [Single band GeoTiff raster holding the
    interpolated flood duration for the event. These files\' information
    are copied in there respective band of the hzd\_\*.tif files.],
    [hzd\_\*.tif], [3-band water hazard GeoTiff raster, where the 3
    bands are (#strong[in this order]): H(x, y), the field of the
    maximum water depth during the event; V(x, y), the field of the
    maximum water speed during the event; and T(x, y) the field of the
    flood duration for this event. For HZD based on slf files, the
    `_from_freesurf` suffix indicates that the maximum depth was
    computed as the difference between the simulated free surface and
    the DEM, rather than directly interpolated from the model.],
    [lcv\_\*.gpkg, lcv\_\*.tif], [Vector (.gpkg) and Raster (.tif)
    landcovers used for the study.],
    [dmgfac\_\*.gpkg, dmgfac\_\*.tif], [Vector (.gpkg) and Raster (.tif)
    \- depending on which landcover was used - damage factor for the JRC
    damage functions. The final damage is the product
    `dmgfac * maxdmg`.],
    [maxdmg\_\*.gpkg, maxdmg\_\*.tif], [Vector (.gpkg) and Raster (.tif)
    \- depending on which landcover was used - maximum damage
    (vulnerability) for the JRC damage functions. The final damage is
    the product `dmgfac * maxdmg`.],
    [std\_\*.gpkg, std\_\*.tif], [Vector (.gpkg) and Raster (.tif) -
    depending on which landcover was used - standard deviation for the
    JRC damage functions. It is often empty depending on the considered
    region.],
    [dmg\_\*.gpkg, dmg\_\*.tif], [Vector (.gpkg) and Raster (.tif) -
    depending on which landcover was used - estimated damage: per vector
    object by the Floodam damage functions (.gpkg, €) and per pixel by
    the JRC damage functions (.tif, €/pixel,
    `dmgfac * maxdmg * pixel area`).],
    [ghslpop\_\*.tif], [GHSL-POP population rasters, where the pixels
    kept correpond to the conditions applied to a specific water hazard
    (see full file name).],
    [filosofi\_\*.gpkg], [Filosofi population pseudo-rasters, where the
    pixels kept correpond to the conditions applied to a specific water
    hazard (see full file name).],
    [\*.png], [Each of the previously mentioned files can be
    automatically plotted for a quick assessment of the code outputs, in
    this case the plot name is just `filename + ".png"`],
  )]
  , kind: table
  )
